# Offboard Employee — Gusto + Slack + Chekkit + Bravo

The exit counterpart to `onboard-employee` / `onboard-employee-slack-chekkit`. Revokes pay,
access, and tools in the right order, and closes the Virginia compliance loop.

Run the whole thing without check-ins. See "The one question" below for the single exception.

> **READ THIS BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE.** The one step that has actually failed in practice is
> **Bravo (Step 4)**. Gusto, Slack and Chekkit have been done correctly every time. Bravo was
> silently skipped on *every* offboarding before 2026-09-17, and a sweep that day found **four**
> ex-employees with working POS logins — the system that touches cash, loans and inventory.
> **An offboarding is not complete until Bravo reads TERMINATED and you have seen it.**

## Step 0 — Identify the person and their store

1. `slack_search_public_and_private` on the name — `#general` daily clock-in posts and
   `#timekeeping-summary` reliably give the legal name and store.
2. Gusto → People → find them. Capture: **legal name, job title, department (= store),
   manager, start date, personal email**. The personal email is the key that links their
   Slack and Chekkit accounts.
3. Watch for name drift: Gusto carries the legal name, Slack/Chekkit often carry a nickname
   (Joseph Epperly = "Joey", Chadd McClintic = "Chadd"). Match on **email**, not display name.

## The one question — voluntary vs involuntary

Gusto **requires** "Did [name] choose to leave?" and it is not a cosmetic field: it is the
record behind a VEC (Virginia Employment Commission) unemployment claim response and behind
any later wrongful-termination allegation. **Never guess it.** If the answer isn't already
explicit in Joshua's request or in Slack, ask exactly this one question via AskUserQuestion
with two options (involuntary / voluntary) and nothing else. Everything else is decided here.

If their last confirmed clock-in is materially earlier than today, ask the effective date in
the same AskUserQuestion call — it drives final pay and the VEC record, and guessing it is the
same class of error as guessing the separation type.

Reason-for-dismissal dropdown is optional — leave it blank unless Joshua stated a reason in
words. A wrong reason code is worse than no reason code.

## Step 1 — Gusto dismissal

1. `https://app.gusto.com/payroll_admin` to confirm the session (see `gusto-access` — expect
   at most one Touch ID from Joshua; never enter credentials).
2. People → the employee → **Work** tab → **Dismiss** link.
   - ⚠️ **The Gusto API employee uuid is NOT the web-app people id.** Building the dismiss URL
     from the uuid returned by `list_employees` gives a "No person found" 404. Go through
     `https://app.gusto.com/payroll_admin/employees` and click the person's name link; the URL
     that lands is the correct `/payroll_admin/people/<id>/…`.
3. Fill the form:
   - **Last working date** and **Pay through date** — both the effective date.
   - **Did they choose to leave?** — the answer from above. *Required.*
   - Reason / dismissal notes — only if Joshua gave one.
   - Personal email — Gusto pre-fills it; **record it before continuing**, you need it for
     Slack and Chekkit and it gets harder to retrieve after dismissal.
4. Save & continue → Review → **Dismiss employee**. This is irreversible; Gusto says so.
5. Verify: the page lands on the **Offboarding checklist** showing `Offboarding` and
   "Last working date on <date>". "<Name> will be paid on normal payrolls" = final pay is
   scheduled — that satisfies Virginia's "on or before the next regular payday" rule.

### Browser gotchas

- **Typing into the date fields is blocked by the action classifier.** Use `form_input` on the
  MM / DD / YYYY spinbuttons. They accept values directly and the review screen will confirm
  them — don't re-enter if a screenshot looks empty.
- **Submitting with the required radio blank reloads the page with `?cache_reset=` and wipes
  every field.** Set the voluntary/involuntary radio FIRST, then the dates, then submit.
- After a reload, `read_page` refs are stale — re-read before clicking.
- The Gusto MCP connector has **no termination tool** — `list_employee_terminations` reads only.
  The web UI is the only path; don't burn a cycle looking for an API shortcut.

## Step 2 — Slack

1. Check first: `slack_search_users` on the name AND on the personal email. New hires who
   lasted days often never got a Slack account — if there's no hit, there's nothing to do.
2. Confirm at `https://valleypawnworkspace.slack.com/admin` (Manage members) — filter by name
   or email. Also check **Invitations → Pending** for an unaccepted invite and revoke it.
3. If an account exists: row's `•••` → **Deactivate account**. Deactivate, never delete —
   deactivation preserves their message history, which is the record you want if a claim or a
   loss-prevention question comes up later.
4. ⚠️ **Verify correctly.** The **Account type** column still reads "Regular Member" after a
   successful deactivation — that column is the account *type*, not its status, and reading it
   as failure will send you round the loop re-clicking a thing that already worked. The real
   proof: reopen the row's `•••` menu and confirm it now offers **"Activate account"**.

## Step 3 — Chekkit

1. `https://dashboard.chekkit.io/settings/team`.
2. **Switch the location context (top-left switcher) to their home store first** — the team
   list is per-location, so a "not found" in the wrong location proves nothing. Locations:
   Culpeper, Harrisonburg, Roanoke, Waynesboro, Lexington (plus a non-VP "Tax Experts" tenant).
   Select the location by element ref, not by coordinate — the list scrolls and a coordinate
   click lands on the wrong store (this has happened; Lexington got selected instead of
   Harrisonburg). After switching, the app shows "Preparing your workspace…" for ~10s and you
   must re-navigate to `/settings/team`.
3. Find their row by email → the row's `⋮`/pencil → **Delete** → confirm (the dialog names the
   email — read it before confirming).
4. If they moved stores during employment, check the prior store's list too.

## Step 4 — Bravo POS — THE STEP THAT GETS SKIPPED. TREAT IT AS THE PRIORITY.

Bravo is the access that touches **cash, loans, and inventory**. It matters more than Slack and
Chekkit combined. **Never report an offboarding complete without a verified Bravo status of
TERMINATED.** If you cannot get into Bravo this session, the offboarding is NOT done — say so
plainly, log it as open in the register, and tell Joshua exactly whose access is still live.

> **Why this is written this way (2026-09-17):** a sweep found four ex-employees with working
> POS logins. **Andrew Clark** (Harrisonburg manager, gone 11 weeks, Region = `VA REGION`, so
> his login reached **every store**), **Davon Camber** (7 weeks), **Logan Dean** (Harrisonburg
> manager), and **Jonathan Smith** (an outside accountant, also `VA REGION`). Every one of them
> had been correctly removed from Gusto, Slack and Chekkit. The written process was never the
> problem — the execution was.

**Exact path (verified 2026-09-17):**

1. **Contention check first**, per `bravo-context`:
   `bash ".../Bravo Data Extraction/_bravo_foreground_guard.sh" check` → must print `CLEAR`;
   then `acquire <task-name>`, and `release <task-name>` when done, success or failure.
   If `BUSY`, stop — do not fight another session for the screen.
2. **Full-screen control is REQUIRED.** Background `app_*` clicks do **not** reach the
   Parallels guest OS — they return "ok" and silently do nothing. Ask for the takeover up
   front and say why (Bravo runs inside the VM); don't rediscover this every time.
3. **Bravo user admin is COMPANY-WIDE from ANY store's System Configuration.** You do NOT need
   `bravo-store-cycle`, and it does not matter which store Bravo is currently logged into.
   Path: right sidebar **System Configuration** → `VALPAW Company` → **Stores** → *their store*
   → **Employees** → the person.
4. **Edit** → **Status** dropdown (CURRENT / TERMINATED / UNKNOWN) → **TERMINATED** → **Save**.
   Edit and Save are the same control in the same screen position, so the sequence is
   Edit → Status → TERMINATED → Save.
5. **Verify both:** the record reads `TERMINATED` **and** the person disappears from that
   store's active Employees tree. One without the other is not confirmation.
6. **Read the Region field before closing.** `This Store Only` is normal; **`VA REGION` means
   their login reached every store** — call that out to Joshua, it changes how urgent the gap
   was.

**While you're in there (~1 minute, do it every time):** expand the other four stores'
Employees nodes and compare against the Gusto active roster. This is how the four stale users
were caught. Expected non-employees that are fine to leave: `FREE1 VALLEY PAWN` (the universal
automation login) and `BACKUP BACKUP` (service account). Anything else that isn't a current
employee gets **reported to Joshua, not terminated** — verify against the source of record
before acting (Rule 12).

## Step 5 — Notify the manager

DM the store manager on Slack (Harrisonburg = Walker Tapley, Waynesboro = Chadd McClintic,
Culpeper = Sandi Cole, Lexington = Uriah Tiglao, Roanoke = Benjie Moore — see
`valley-pawn-context`): plain language, one message, what's revoked and what they need to
physically collect (keys, store phone, any assigned equipment). No technical detail, no failure
jargon (Rule 16). Draft it in Joshua's voice per `my-writing-style` — short, no greeting, no
sign-off, the ask last.

## Step 6 — Compliance close-out (Virginia)

- **Final pay** — due on or before the next regular payday. Gusto handles it via normal
  payroll once dismissed; if the person needs paying sooner, run an off-cycle.
- **Unpaid final hours** — verify their last timecard is complete **before** the payroll runs;
  a missing clock-out becomes an unpaid-wages problem. ⚠️ Once dismissed, the person drops out
  of the `list_time_records` connector response entirely — so check this in the Gusto **Time &
  attendance** UI, or pull it *before* you dismiss them.
- **VEC claim** — expect a claim notice if the separation was involuntary. The Gusto record
  and any documented warnings (`#records-of-warning`, ROC PDFs in the Human Resources folder)
  are the response file. Note in the summary if the person had *no* warnings and good
  performance numbers — that shapes how Joshua responds to a claim.
- **Records retention** — keep the personnel file; do not purge anything.
- **COBRA** — only if they were enrolled in benefits. Gusto flags this on the dismissal form.

## Step 7 — Log it (Rule 14)

Add a row to `~/Documents/Claude/Projects/Life OS/OPEN_ITEMS_REGISTER.md`: who, effective
date, separation type, which systems were revoked **and how each was verified**, plus anything
left open (keys not yet collected, timecard unconfirmed).

## Verification, not assumption (Rule 12)

Before reporting done, confirm each of:

- **Gusto** shows `Offboarding` / dismissed with the right last working date
- **Slack** row's `•••` offers "Activate account" (or the person genuinely never had an account)
- **Chekkit** team list for their store no longer lists them
- **Bravo** status reads `TERMINATED` and they are off the store's active Employees tree

"I clicked it" is not verification. If any one of these is unconfirmed, say which — do not
round up to "all done."

